# Line endings are not cosmetic in this repository.
#
# Almost everything here is a shell script that runs on Linux or inside a
# container. Git for Windows checks files out as CRLF by default, and a shell
# script with CRLF endings fails as:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which tells the reader nothing useful. Worse, `.env` files and the Docker
# entrypoints would carry stray carriage returns into container environments,
# where they turn a password into "secret\r" and the database refuses the login.
#
# So: normalise to LF on commit AND on checkout, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The port patch must survive byte for byte -- `patch` counts characters, and a
# rewritten line ending makes every hunk fail to apply.
*.patch -text

# The game's Lua/text loaders in this tree expect the package's own CRLF
# exactly (see the Dockerfile's own `sed -i 's/$/\r/'` steps on siblings of
# these files); LF-normalising them on checkout would silently corrupt the
# translate tables and locale strings the game boots from.
linux-port-mt2009/docker/game/src/serverfiles/share/locale/poland/translate.lua -text
linux-port-mt2009/docker/game/src/serverfiles/share/locale/poland/quest/libs/translate/translate.lua -text
linux-port-mt2009/docker/game/src/serverfiles/share/locale/poland/locale_string.txt -text

# world.sql (the mob_proto/item_proto DB seed) is cp1250, mostly LF, but a
# handful of NPC names carry a literal embedded CRLF as part of the value
# itself (not a line terminator) -- eol=lf normalisation would silently
# rewrite those bytes inside the SQL string literal.
linux-port-mt2009/docker/mariadb/initdb.d/dumps/world.sql -text

# Binary, never transform.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz  binary
